# Claude-Context — read this first

You are an agent working on the **AsterMind ELM integration** into the n-dx codebase.
Three interns work this repo in parallel. This file tells you where things live, what
you own, and what you must not touch. Read it fully before your first tool call.

---

## 1. The project in one paragraph

n-dx is an AI dev toolkit (sourcevision / rex / hench) that currently pays a hosted LLM
(Claude, OpenAI, Gemini) for every inference. Our job is to replace those LLM dependencies
with free, local **ELMs** (Extreme Learning Machines) via `@astermind/astermind-community`.
Not every call can be replaced — classification-shaped calls can, open-ended generation
mostly cannot. Deciding which is which is half the work, and those decisions go in ADRs.

Prior art already in `main`: commit `43d6db51` adds the dependency and
`scripts/elm-hello-world.mjs`, a seeded smoke test that trains an ELM on file-path
archetype classification under Node. **Read that script before proposing anything** — it
documents two library gotchas that will otherwise cost you an hour:
- `charSet` is interpolated unescaped into a RegExp character class, so a literal `-`
  must come last or it forms an invalid range and throws.
- Text training requires `useTokenizer: true`.
- npm's latest is `3.0.0`; the repo pins `^3.0.0`. v4 is tagged on GitHub but unpublished
  and breaking. Do not chase v4.

## 2. Folder layout

```
Claude-Context/
  claude-context-instruction   <- this file. The index. Always read first.
  ADR/                         <- development plans: decisions, with reasoning
  IMPL/                        <- implementation plans: step-by-step, per decision
  Claude-Agents/               <- one file per intern's agent; its own work history
  OWNERSHIP.md                 <- who owns which paths. Check before editing.
  IN-FLIGHT.md                 <- what is being edited right now. Claim before starting.
```

**ADR** — *Why we are doing it this way.* One decision per file, numbered, immutable once
merged. If a decision changes, write a new ADR that supersedes the old one; don't rewrite
history. Template: `ADR/ADR-000-template.md`.

**IMPL** — *How we will do it.* A plan derived from one or more ADRs: files to touch,
order of operations, test strategy, rollback. Mutable — update as reality intrudes.
Template: `IMPL/IMPL-template.md`.

**Claude-Agents** — *What has actually happened.* One file per intern, named for that
intern's agent. Append-only log of sessions: what you did, what you learned, what broke,
what's next. This is the context-reclaim mechanism — a fresh agent reads its own file and
resumes without re-deriving anything.

## 3. Your operating loop

At the **start** of every session:
1. Read this file.
2. Read your own `Claude-Agents/<YOUR-NAME>.md` — that is your memory.
3. Read `OWNERSHIP.md` and `IN-FLIGHT.md` — know what is yours and what is hot.
4. `git fetch && git status` — know where you are.

At the **end** of every session (or before context runs out, whichever comes first):
1. Append a session entry to `Claude-Agents/<YOUR-NAME>.md` using the format in that file.
2. Remove or update your `IN-FLIGHT.md` claim.
3. Commit. An uncommitted agent log is a lost agent log.

If you are given a name that has no file in `Claude-Agents/`, copy
`Claude-Agents/AGENT-TEMPLATE.md` to `Claude-Agents/<name>.md` and start there.

## 4. Rules that prevent collisions

These are not style preferences. Violating them destroys another intern's work.

**Branches.** Never commit to `main`. Branch as `elm/<your-name>/<short-topic>`.
Prefer a `git worktree` per intern so three agents are not sharing one dirty checkout:
`git worktree add ../n-dx-<yourname> -b elm/<yourname>/<topic>`.

**PRD state is not concurrency-safe.** The repo's own `CLAUDE.md` documents this: any
command that writes `.rex/prd_tree/`, `.sourcevision/`, or `.hench/config.json` must not
run concurrently with another such command. The last writer wins *silently* — no error,
just data loss. That means: **do not run `ndx plan`, `ndx work`, `ndx ci`, `ndx refresh`,
`ndx self-heal`, or rex MCP write tools without claiming it in `IN-FLIGHT.md` first.**
Read-only (`ndx status`, `ndx usage`) is always safe. If you are in your own worktree with
its own `.rex/`, this constraint is local to you — say so in your claim.

**Ownership before editing.** `OWNERSHIP.md` assigns paths. If you need a file you don't
own, don't edit it — open an entry in `IN-FLIGHT.md` describing the change you need and
say so in your handoff. The shared hot spots are, in order of danger:
`packages/llm-client/src/provider-registry.ts`, `provider-interface.ts`, `llm-types.ts`,
`llm-config.ts`, `package.json`, `pnpm-lock.yaml`.

**The ELM provider is a registry entry, not a fork.** `ProviderRegistry.register(vendor,
factory)` (`packages/llm-client/src/provider-registry.ts`) is the extension seam. Add ELM
as a vendor through it. Do not modify existing provider files to bolt ELM in — that
guarantees three-way merge conflicts, because all three of us have reason to touch them.

**Architecture rules still apply.** Read the root `CLAUDE.md`. The tier hierarchy
(orchestration -> hench -> rex/sourcevision -> llm-client) is enforced by tests in
`tests/e2e/`. Cross-package imports go through gateway modules only. Run
`pnpm typecheck && pnpm test` before you claim anything is done.

**Numbering.** ADRs and IMPLs use reserved ranges per intern (see `OWNERSHIP.md`) so two
people never mint `ADR-004` at the same time.

**pnpm-lock.yaml.** Adding a dependency touches the lockfile, which conflicts brutally.
Announce dependency additions in `IN-FLIGHT.md` before running `pnpm add`. Resolve
lockfile conflicts by re-running `pnpm install`, never by hand-merging.

**Changesets.** Default every bump to `patch` unless told otherwise (root `CLAUDE.md`).
Changeset filenames are random, so they don't collide — one changeset per PR is fine.

## 5. Honesty requirements

Your agent log is used by other people to decide what to build next. Therefore:
- If tests fail, write down that they failed, with output. Do not write "done".
- If you skipped part of a task, say which part and why.
- If an ELM turned out to be a bad fit for a call site, that is a **finding**, not a
  failure — record it in an ADR. A documented "we tried, here's the accuracy, it's not
  good enough" is worth more than a quiet workaround.
- Report accuracy numbers with the seed and the baseline. "It works" is not a result.
